1	//! `RepoSession`: an in-process working session with one repo.
2	//!
3	//! The plumbing jj's own CLI wraps around every mutation
4	//! (`WorkspaceCommandHelper` in jj-cli), reduced to what the facade's
5	//! verbs need: user-config-backed settings, the working-copy snapshot
6	//! cycle, and transaction finish. The reference implementation is jj
7	//! 0.43's `cli_util.rs`, with deviations documented on each function.
8	//! The name is backend-neutral on purpose: what a session holds and
9	//! its open -> mutate -> finish lifecycle would survive a non-jj
10	//! backend.
11	//!
12	//! - `RepoSession::open`: settings + workspace + repo-at-head, colocation
13	//!   detected.
14	//! - `RepoSession::snapshot`: the CLI's `maybe_snapshot`: git head/refs
15	//!   import around a working-copy snapshot, under the same lock file
16	//!   the CLI takes.
17	//! - `RepoSession::finish_tx`: the CLI's `finish_transaction`: git HEAD
18	//!   reset + ref export, op-store commit, working-copy update.
19	//! - The publish-path verbs (`commit`, `describe`, `bookmark_set`,
20	//!   `git_push_bookmark`, `git_fetch`) and the repositioning verbs
21	//!   (`new_on`, `rebase_branch`): each snapshots, mutates in
22	//!   one transaction, and finishes (one op per verb, so the op log
23	//!   keeps the shape push re-run and manual op restore rely on).
24	//!
25	//! A session lives as long as its owner wants: `Context` caches one
26	//! per repo for a whole invocation, the facade's one-shot wrappers
27	//! open one per call. Either way each verb starts with `snapshot`,
28	//! which reloads at the op-store head, so a cached session never
29	//! acts on a stale view.
30	//!
31	//! The colocated git writes (HEAD/index reset, ref export) retry on
32	//! lockfile contention (`retry_git_lock`): a git-aware watcher can
33	//! hold `.git/index.lock` at the moment we reset the index, and gix
34	//! gives the lock a single attempt (bugs.md #1).
35	
